  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1129 AM CST MON NOV 23 2026\n\nThe cold front moving through the region today is currently\nsituated near the I-59 corridor, with continued progression south\ninto the overnight hours. Winds are quickly expected to shift\nnorth behind this front, ushering in cool, dry air overnight\ntonight. Temperatures are expected to remain cooler into\nWednesday, even though upper-level ridging briefly builds in on\nTuesday ahead of the next system. This will be in part due to a\nstout CAD wedge, that will shift winds to the E/NE, keeping that\ncolder air situated across the region.\n\nBy Wednesday, the second, more potent system in this forecast\nperiod is still expected to work through the region, bringing\nwith it even colder temperatures and increased rain chances.\nHere, a stout trough is still expected to be working south\nthrough the Plains, becoming a cutoff low somewhere near E.\nTexas. Ahead of this closed low, flow aloft will switch back to\nthe SW, allowing for quick moisture returns in the upper-levels.\nAt the surface however, winds will remain from the E/NE, with\nrelatively dry conditions abound. The bulk of the moisture with\nthis next system will be found in the upper-levels, with PWAT\nvalues ranging between 1 and 1.25 inches. Thankfully, given the\nstout forcing with this closed low, most of this upper-level\nmoisture should still find its way to the surface in the form of\nrain. Most locations can expect to see between a half and inch,\nto an inch of rainfall through Wednesday night. These rainfall\ntotals may increase in the coming days, depending on how deep\nthat moisture return is ahead of this system.\n\nBy Thanksgiving proper, the bulk of this rainfall should have\nexited the region, with low chances for a lingering shower or\ntwo. However, most locations should remain dry during\nThanksgiving. Otherwise, seasonal to slightly below seasonal\ntemperatures are expected into the weekend, with dry conditions\nprevailing through Saturday.\n\n/44/",
